course: dictionary
lesson: 7330
title: Planetesimals: the building blocks of planets
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things nobody can see up close, and planetesimal is one of them. Break it into clues. The word "planet" tells you it is about planets, and it is a noun, so it names a thing. «In the disc, dust grains stuck together into pebbles, then rocks, then bodies kilometres wide called planetesimals.» The sentence says they are bodies, bigger than rocks, that crashed and merged into planets.
explain: Look at the order in that sentence: dust, then pebbles, then rocks, then planetesimals, then planets. Each step is bigger than the one before, so a planetesimal sits between a rock and a planet in size. Swap the meaning into the sentence to check: "bodies kilometres wide" fits a lump of rock, dust or ice, so the clue agrees with the meaning.
explain: Scientists think planetesimals helped form the planets of our Solar System about 4.6 billion years ago. Asteroids and comets may be leftover planetesimals that never joined a planet. The phrase "dust grows into planetesimals" shows the change from tiny grains to larger lumps.
fact: A planetesimal is a small solid lump of rock, dust or ice.
fact: Planetesimals are from a few metres to a few kilometres across.
fact: Planetesimals moved around a young star.
fact: Scientists think many planetesimals crashed and stuck together to build planets.
fact: Asteroids and comets may be leftover planetesimals.
exercise: «In the disc, dust grains stuck together into pebbles, then rocks, then bodies kilometres wide called planetesimals.» What is a planetesimal? | A bright star that never moves | A tiny grain of sand on a beach | *A small solid lump of rock, dust or ice that moved around a young star | A giant gas planet with rings
exercise: Scientists think planetesimals were the building blocks of what? | Forests | Rain clouds | *Planets | Deserts
exercise: Find and copy one word from the sentence that describes grains sticking together. | planetesimals | pebbles | disc | *stuck
test: Put these in order from smallest to biggest, as in the sentence. | rocks, dust, planets, pebbles | planetesimals, dust, rocks, pebbles | *dust, pebbles, rocks, planetesimals
test: Scientists think asteroids and comets may be what? | Small planets with air | Moons of Jupiter | *Leftover planetesimals | Hot balls of gas
exam: A planetesimal is best described as: | A tiny star | A cloud of rain | *A solid lump of rock, dust or ice around a young star | A ring of gas around Saturn
